.TH BASHMENU 1 "2026-09-08" "v0.0.1" "HA Bash Menu"
.SH NAME
bashmenu \- lightweight curses TUI menu engine and setting editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bashmenu.sh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bashmenu
is a data-driven, Curses-based Text User Interface (TUI) menu engine designed
for Linux system administration, setting management, and command execution. It
reads its menu structure from a YAML-formatted menu file and dynamically
interpolates configurations and system variables.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bashmenu.sh
Launcher shell script that auto-initializes and repairs Python virtualenv.
.TP
.I bashmenu.py
Core Python application containing Curses renderer and menu engine.
.TP
.I menuedit.py
Interactive TUI menu tree editor for managing bashmenu.mnu.
.TP
.I requirements.txt
Project Python dependencies (specifically PyYAML).
.TP
.I bashmenu.yml
Settings configuration file store in YAML format.
.TP
.I bashmenu.mnu
Menu layout structure definition and item directives.
.TP
.I bashmenu.themes
Color theme palette definitions (256, 16, and 8 color modes).
.SH KEYBOARD SHORTCUTS
.SS Menu Navigation
.TP
.B UP / DOWN / j / k
Navigate selected menu row.
.TP
.B 0-9 / a-z / A-Z
Select menu item directly by shortcut hotkey.
.TP
.B F4
Launch menuedit.py visual menu editor directly from main menu.
.TP
.B F5
Toggle visibility of menu item shortcut key badges.
.TP
.B ESC
Return to parent menu, or quit if at root.
.SS Visual Menu Editor (menuedit.py)
.TP
.B a
Insert new menu option node.
.TP
.B e / ENTER
Edit node properties or menu header title.
.TP
.B E
Open raw YAML snippet editor for the selected node.
.TP
.B d / Delete
Delete selected menu option node (with confirmation).
.TP
.B m / M
Move selected item down (m) or up (M).
.TP
.B < / >
Outdent (<) item to parent menu or indent (>) item into preceding Submenu.
.TP
.B t
Test-run selected menu item action directly.
.TP
.B s
Save changes to bashmenu.mnu (creates .bak backup).
.SS Built-in Text Editor
The application features a built-in, Nano-style terminal text editor for
editing target files. It supports advanced features such as multi-level
undo/redo, text selection, copy/cut/paste, line numbers, whitespace visibility
toggles, and direct integration with file pickers.
.TP
.B Ctrl+X
Exit editor (prompts to save if modifications exist).
.TP
.B Ctrl+O / F3
WriteOut (saves the active file).
.TP
.B Ctrl+R / F5
Read File (opens file picker modal to insert file content or select new file).
.TP
.B Ctrl+S / F6
Save As (prompts for destination target file path).
.TP
.B Ctrl+N / F4
Create a new empty document.
.TP
.B Ctrl+G / F1
Toggle bottom Nano-style shortcut keys help bar.
.TP
.B Ctrl+K / F8
Cut line or highlighted text block.
.TP
.B Ctrl+U / F9
Paste (uncut) cutbuffer text.
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Show cursor location, or copy selection if selection mark is active.
.B Alt+6
Copy line or highlighted text block.
.TP
.B Alt+U
Undo the last edit operation.
.TP
.B Alt+E
Redo the last undone edit operation.
.TP
.B Ctrl+^ / Alt+A
Set/unset text selection mark.
.TP
.B Alt+N
Toggle line numbers gutter display.
.TP
.B Alt+P / Alt+W
Toggle whitespace character visibility.
.TP
.B F2
Launch external system $EDITOR (e.g. nano or vim) as a fallback.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings are persistently stored in the
.I bashmenu.yml
file. Value lookups can use dot-notation (e.g. settings.dns.ip).
.SH DIRECTIVES
Menu items in
.I bashmenu.mnu
support the following attributes:
.TP
.B type
Option type. Supported values are:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B command
- Executes a shell command line string.
.IP \(bu 2
.B script
- Runs a script file from the application folder.
.IP \(bu 2
.B config
- Edits a bashmenu.yml value.
.IP \(bu 2
.B param
- Input text passed to a script.
.IP \(bu 2
.B toggle
- Launches an interactive True/False/Cancel configuration toggle modal.
.IP \(bu 2
.B editor
- Opens a file in the built-in Nano-style editor.
.IP \(bu 2
.B confirm
- Launches a Yes/No confirmation dialog.
.IP \(bu 2
.B message
- Displays an information message popup.
.IP \(bu 2
.B python
- Runs a registered built-in Python routine.
.IP \(bu 2
.B inject_block
- Injects/updates a dynamic configuration block using a template.
.IP \(bu 2
.B theme_selector
- Displays a dynamic theme selection menu.
.IP \(bu 2
.B back
- Navigates back to the parent menu.
.IP \(bu 2
.B exit
- Exits the application.
.IP \(bu 2
.B submenu
- Opens a nested option submenu folder.
.RE
.TP
.B label
Display string shown in the menu row.
.TP
.B icon
Separate icon/emoji string utilizing the nf:FALLBACK:GLYPH format.
.TP
.B action
Command string, script path, or editor file path.
.TP
.B template
Source template path (required for inject_block).
.TP
.B target
Destination target file path (required for inject_block).
.TP
.B block_id
Unique identifier string for block boundary tags (required for inject_block).
.TP
.B quiet
Whether to suppress output execution headers and pauses.
.TP
.B refresh
Whether to reload environment/menu structure on action return.
.TP
.B stream
Whether to stream command output in a real-time log window.
.TP
.B interactive
Whether to suspend Curses for full terminal interaction.
.TP
.B external
Whether to launch scripts in a separate process (true by default). If set to
false, Python scripts containing a main(stdscr) function run in the same process.
.TP
.B user_mode
Privilege mode ("root" or "user").
.TP
.B key
YAML setting dot-notation key path to write.
.TP
.B picker
Visual chooser mode ("file", "dir", or "none").
.TP
.B start_dir
Starting path for the file/folder picker.
.TP
.B masked
Whether to mask password fields during input.
.SH DYNAMIC VARIABLES
The following tokens are resolved dynamically at runtime:
.TP
.B {user} / {username}
Current system username.
.TP
.B {home}
User's home directory path.
.TP
.B {bashrc} / {vimrc} / {bash_aliases}
Standard user configuration file paths.
.TP
.B {bashmenu_dir}
Path to the active application scripts folder.
.TP
.B {settings.path}
Any setting loaded from bashmenu.yml (using dot-notation).
.TP
.B nf:FALLBACK:GLYPH / {nf:FALLBACK:GLYPH}
Dynamic font/emoji resolution based on settings.use_nerd_fonts.
If enabled, resolves to GLYPH, which can be a raw character or a hex code
prefixed with '#' (e.g. #f0a4 or #f07c0). If disabled, resolves to FALLBACK
(or "" empty string, which collapses the icon column for compact, perfect left alignment).
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Creating a Block Template File
A code block template file (e.g. templates/aliases.tmpl) can contain any
standard shell scripts, configuration statements, or text blocks. You can embed
standard placeholder tokens or dynamic config setting paths using dot-notation.
These values will be fully resolved and expanded upon injection.

Example Template File (templates/aliases.tmpl):
.EX
  # Dynamic system alias definitions for user {user}
  alias my-ip="echo 'Your IP is: {settings.dns.ip}'"
  alias edit-menu="bashmenu.sh"
.EE
.SS Dynamic Block Injection Directive
Inject and manage custom alias configuration block inside .bashrc:
.EX
  - label: Configure Custom Aliases
    type: inject_block
    template: "templates/aliases.tmpl"
    target: "{bashrc}"
    block_id: "custom_aliases"
    refresh: true
.EE
.PP
When selected, if the block is missing, the user is prompted to install it. If
the block is present, the user can reinstall/update it (Yes), cleanly uninstall
it (No), or cancel.
